  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe approach aims to find a step function h that minimizes the maximum overlap integral C\u2085 by iteratively adjusting h to maximize the minimum autocorrelation across all shifts. This method leverages the non-convex optimization landscape by using gradient descent with feasibility projection to guide h towards an optimal configuration. The key insight is to transform the problem into an autocorrelation maximization task, which is tackled through gradient-based updates while maintaining constraints.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nWe define the step function h: [0, 2] \u2192 [0, 1] with n_points samples. The integral constraint is \u2211h[i] * dx = 1, where dx = 2/n_points. The objective is to minimize C\u2085 = max_k ( \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x+k)) dx ). This is equivalent to maximizing the minimum autocorrelation AC(k), where AC(k) = \u222bh(x)h(x+k) dx. The optimization problem is non-convex due to the product terms, but an iterative gradient approach can navigate the solution space.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\n   a. Initialization:\n      - Set n_points = 256 for higher resolution.\n      - Initialize h using initial_h_values or a normalized random vector.\n      - Normalize h to ensure \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2.\n   b. Optimization loop:\n      1. Compute overlap for all shifts using np.correlate.\n      2. Identify k_max with the maximum overlap (smallest AC(k)).\n      3. Compute gradient \u0394h based on contributions at k_max.\n      4. Update h using h = h - learning_rate * \u0394h.\n      5. Project h to [0,1] and normalize to meet sum constraint.\n      6. Repeat until time budget exhausted or convergence criteria met.\n   c. Gradient computation:\n      - For the selected k_max, calculate the gradient as the contribution of each h[i] to the overlap.\n   d. Learning rate adjustment:\n      - Start with a base learning rate (e.g., 0.1).\n      - Adaptively decrease based on gradient norm or time remaining.\n   e. Feasibility projection:\n      - Clip h to [0,1].\n      - Scale to ensure sum(h) = n_points / 2 by adjusting all elements proportionally.\n\n4. Search choices and variations\n- Initial configurations include using initial_h_values or generating a normalized binary pattern.\n- Learning rate can be adjusted with a decay factor based on iteration or time elapsed.\n- Periodically perturb h to escape local minima (e.g., every 100 iterations with a small random change).\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\n- Each iteration takes approximately 1ms, allowing up to 1e6 iterations within the 1100s budget.\n- Gradient computations and projections are optimized using numpy for speed.\n- Robustness is maintained through feasibility checks after each update and periodic perturbations.\n\n6. Validation and return contract\n- Validate h by checking 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 and sum(h) = n_points / 2.\n- Re-compute C\u2085 using the correlation method to ensure the objective is correctly minimized.\n- Return the best h found along with its C\u2085 value and n_points.",
